course: dictionary
lesson: 14519
title: Perdition: the road to ruin
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Some words belong to old books, church talk and classic stories. Perdition is one of them. It is a noun, and it names a terrible fate. «The villain laughed as he sent the hero down the road to perdition.» The road shows that this is a journey towards something bad, and the villain's laugh shows that it is very bad indeed.
explain: Perdition has two meanings. The first, from old church language, is everlasting punishment after death, often meaning hell. The second, which is old and formal, is complete ruin or disaster. «The flood brought perdition to the little farm.» The farm was not sent to hell; it was completely destroyed. Read the words around the word to tell which meaning fits.
explain: Today the word sounds old-fashioned or dramatic, so you will mostly meet it in old Bible translations, hymns and classic stories. Writers use it to warn the reader that a fate is terrible. When you meet perdition, swap in your meaning and check that the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Perdition is a noun, and it is uncountable: you say «perdition», not «a perdition».
fact: «Perdition» means everlasting punishment after death, often meaning hell, in old Christian language.
fact: «Perdition» can also mean complete ruin or disaster, in old and formal writing.
fact: «The road to perdition» is an old phrase for a path that leads to a terrible end.
fact: Today «perdition» sounds old-fashioned or dramatic, so use it carefully in your own writing.
exercise: «The villain laughed as he sent the hero down the road to perdition.» What does the phrase suggest? | *A journey towards a terrible end | A happy holiday | A short cut through the woods | A walk to the seaside
exercise: «The flood brought perdition to the little farm.» What does perdition mean here? | A new and shiny tractor | A party for the farm animals | A gentle spring shower | *Complete ruin and disaster
exercise: Ella says perdition is a happy word for a party. Why is she wrong? | It is a Scottish word for rain | *It names a terrible fate, not something happy | It is a kind of cake | It means a very long holiday
test: In the old hymn, the singer prayed to be kept from perdition. What is perdition most likely to mean here? | A happy feast | A kind of rainy weather | *Everlasting punishment after death, often hell | A school trip
test: Which sentence uses perdition in its formal sense of complete ruin? | *«The storm brought perdition to the harbour town.» | «The museum opens at nine.» | «The children sang a cheerful song.» | «The bakery was saved by its new owner.»
exam: Which is the best meaning of perdition? | A bright light in the sky | A cheap meal | A short holiday | *Complete ruin or everlasting punishment
